# Independent finite Vault recycle review

The reviewed work-only candidate is `work/keepass-recycle-next`, validation `8911d007286682869c3346319e17e58342a730ba5af995d7af32c1faa684b0e1`, manifest `769523b00c626762f18aec86ee8df8f1bb317ff4bbc8147a09a03af44161c100`. All 546 manifest artifacts and the exact inventory were authenticated before and after this review. Production and author stage bytes were unchanged. The frozen Notes325 stage was also authenticated before this task; this is identity verification of my own Notes work, not an independent behavior review.

Fresh finite checks: the five actual-client/actual-server DOM transport cases produced two passes and three failures. Six additional native/API/original comparison groups passed. The three failures are one P2 bin-view generation defect: Restore or Purge checks its bin generation before awaiting the active-entry list, then calls `binList()` using only the overall view guard. Closing the bin during that await reopens it, and a newer bin preview can be erased by the older operation. Detailed synthetic call traces are retained under `observations/`; no HTTP content loss or wrong persisted entry was demonstrated. The two held-response controls verify detached preview suppression and newer draft retention after entry deletion.

The additional six groups cover unknown native XML/history preservation, full-document token invalidation, failed durable Restore/Purge atomicity, ordered recovery with stale purge rejection, lock/reopen persistence, and a held-out existing-bin descendant fixture. Four fresh SHA-authenticated KeePass2.61.1 library processes accepted/read the candidate output and matched UUID ancestry, previous parent, history, timestamps and tombstone semantics. The delete/restore orchestration is source-composed with fixed clocks; original GUI executions are zero.

The author final original accounting was independently reconstructed from raw process results: prior14+1+9 and new14 =38 loads; new14 include direct Touch2, private EnsureRecycleBin2, source-composed Delete5/Restore1 and clock assignments17. The historical failed reflection load is inferred from its exact retained helper (LoadFrom occurs before Invoke) and released PwDatabase conversion error, rather than a success-result count. Its historical reported7/actual8 correction remains preserved unchanged. Fourteen author run receipt identities were checked. No original broad replay was run.

The full official source archive SHA/size and central-directory1381 entries were checked; all16 selected source/license files were matched against their actual archive bytes. The12 exact source spans were authenticated in the before audit. This remains a GPL-2.0-or-later source-assisted finite study. Released default KDBX4.0 drops previous-parent metadata; an actual QualityCheck=false feature forces4.1 and retains it. Candidate exports4.1; this difference is recorded, not treated as default-release equality. Bin unused-expiry clock, legacy ID-only delete, entry-only scope and the author's resource bounds remain explicit limitations.

Actual Chrome proof used one owned temporary shared host at an ephemeral port and synthetic data. Confirmed entry deletion, read-only masked bin preview, confirmed Restore, active previous group/history and lock/reopen persistence passed. Three screenshots and complete accessibility states are preserved. The initial unrelated Editor module was absent from the minimal fixture; Vault loaded normally. No original GUI/browser comparison was made. The tab was closed. Host stdin was unavailable in non-TTY execution; a separately authenticated owned PID was stopped and its verified confined temporary directory removed. No user4311/root hosts were accessed.

Review-harness failures are preserved: the initial inventory audit omitted the excluded `freeze.json`; the final historical audit initially used the wrong observation filename, then initially counted only successful result/marker loads and exposed the known failed-reflection correction. Those source versions/raw failures remain under `failures/`. A quoting-only inline shell replacement failed before any file edit; the intended replacement was then applied with a structured patch. No candidate defect is attributed to these harness errors.

Finite acceptance: native checks and the normal browser workflow pass, but this candidate is not accepted for integration until the three stale bin-publication cases are fixed in a separate stage and independently rerun. No full compatibility, security, performance or superiority claim is made.
